---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Passage = `THE LOST PET FINDER

A veterinary assistant wrote a small program for her own town.
Anyone who found a stray animal could post a photo of it, and
anyone who had lost one could post a photo too. The program
compared the photos and suggested matches.

She shared it with two neighboring towns. Within eighteen months
it was in use in four hundred towns and by several million
people. She still maintains it alone, in the evenings.

Photos taken on a phone usually carry the place they were taken.
The program reads that and ranks nearby matches first, which is
why matches are found quickly. It also means a poster's
neighborhood can be worked out from a photo they uploaded from
home.

Animal shelters began using the posts differently. A shelter that
takes in an animal can search old posts to find the person who
first reported it missing, and some shelters have used that to
bill those owners for boarding costs. The assistant did not plan
this and cannot prevent it.

Rescue groups say the program has cut the time an animal spends
in a shelter. Some owners say they stopped reporting animals
missing once they heard about the billing.

Every post stays visible until the poster deletes it. Posts from
the first year are still online.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 17: Reading Passage</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Five questions &middot; one passage &middot; 12 minutes
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Due session 56, while you are building.</b> There is no code in
            this one and nothing to run. Read it, answer it, hand it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Five of the seventy questions on the AP exam share one passage about
            a computing innovation, and reasoning about computing innovations
            is{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">28&ndash;33% of the multiple-choice section</b>{" "}
            &mdash; more than any other skill, including writing code.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>You have not been taught this content yet, and you do not need
            to have been.</b> Everything the questions ask about is in the
            passage. We come back to it properly in Part 3.
          </p>

          <h4>How to Do This One</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Read the whole passage first.</b> All of it, before you look at
              a single question.
            </li>
            <li><b>Set a timer for 12 minutes.</b> The reading is part of it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Every answer is in the passage or follows from it.</b> Nothing
              here needs outside knowledge about animal shelters. If you find
              yourself using something you already believed, check that the
              passage actually says it.
            </li>
            <li>Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, as always.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="passage">
          <h2>The Passage</h2>

          <Code
            code={Passage}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Read this before looking at the questions"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>
            The program reads the place a photo was taken and uses it to rank
            nearby matches first. Which statement is best supported by the
            passage?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) These are two separate features, one that helps and one that harms</li>
            <li>(B) One feature produces both the quick matches and the exposure of a poster's neighborhood</li>
            <li>(C) The exposure is a defect that careful testing would have caught before release</li>
            <li>(D) The feature is harmful only in cases where the poster does not know about it</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>
            The program went from one town to four hundred in eighteen months,
            and is still maintained by one person in the evenings. Which is best
            supported?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Growing that fast is evidence the program was not properly designed</li>
            <li>(B) The assistant is obliged to stop maintaining it until she can do so full time</li>
            <li>(C) Impacts at that scale are the responsibility of the shelters, not of the program</li>
            <li>(D) A large number of users can produce impacts beyond what the person who wrote it intended or can control</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which of these are supported by the passage as effects the assistant{" "}
            <b>did not plan</b>?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Shelters billing owners for boarding costs</li>
            <li>(B) A poster's neighborhood being worked out from a photo they uploaded</li>
            <li>(C) Some owners stopping reporting their animals missing</li>
            <li>(D) Photos being compared so that matches can be suggested</li>
            <li>(E) Animals spending less time in shelters than before</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>
            A shelter searches old posts to find the person who first reported an
            animal missing, then bills them for boarding. This is best described
            as:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) a fault in the program, since it allows searches it was not built for</li>
            <li>(B) plagiarism, since the shelter is using material someone else created</li>
            <li>(C) an innovation being used in a way its creator did not intend</li>
            <li>(D) a privacy breach, since the shelter has access it was never granted</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            Someone argues that the assistant should have foreseen the billing
            use before she released the program. Which response is best supported
            by the passage?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) She should have, because a responsible developer considers every possible use</li>
            <li>(B) She could not have listed every use in advance, and the passage still shows uses appearing that she now has to answer for</li>
            <li>(C) Foreseeing it was impossible, so how the program gets used is no longer her concern</li>
            <li>(D) The billing is not an effect of the program, because the program was not built to do it</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Five answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off. Worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on
            completion.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And one written answer, in a sentence or two:</b> the passage says
            posts from the first year are still online. Name one way that could
            matter to someone who posted back then, using only what the passage
            tells you.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
